WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.440
[Upbeat Music]

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:10.678
My lab at NLM is studying the non-coding&nbsp;
part of the human genome. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.678 --> 00:00:21.440
When the human genome was sequenced in 2001, what we learned&nbsp;
is that only 2% of the sequence of our genome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.287
is used for coding genes. That in turn translate into proteins. 

00:00:27.287 --> 00:00:31.566
This is the sequence information&nbsp;that's being used by every single&nbsp;cell.&nbsp;

00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:39.751
&nbsp;
But it only represents 2% of our genome.&nbsp;&nbsp;98% of our DNA is not being used.&nbsp;

00:00:39.751 --> 00:00:42.080
There&nbsp;were some speculation suggesting that

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:50.160
it could be that that large chunk of our genome&nbsp;
is purely junk and, in reality, that’s not true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:57.280
There are treasure islands, within the sea&nbsp;
of non-coding DNA in the human genome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.185
that are critically important for regulating the genes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.905 --> 00:01:07.200
These are the pieces that we&nbsp;can refer to as light switches.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.880
What we’ve learned, is&nbsp;95%&nbsp;of disease associations,&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.200
they hide within these&nbsp;non-coding&nbsp;part of our genomes.&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:25.560
And what my lab is&nbsp;trying to do is look
through these vast landscapes&nbsp;of the human genome&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.401
and identify where these&nbsp;light switches are&nbsp;hiding &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.401 --> 00:01:34.000
and to figure out what exactly do they do.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.160
Which genes do they&nbsp;turn on,
when do they turn these genes on,&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.565
and what happens if they&nbsp;don’t turn these genes on?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.565 --> 00:01:44.668
So, if we want to understand &nbsp;

00:01:44.668 --> 00:01:49.040
how we can&nbsp;address human diseases and disorders,&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.120
we have to study and to understand&nbsp;
the non-coding part&nbsp;of our genomes.&nbsp;

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:03.520
I still cannot comprehend how we can&nbsp;
conquer space, have self-driving cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.122
and do not understand the encryption of the human genome. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.122 --> 00:02:16.434
I am really interested in trying to get to this hidden second code of our&nbsp;genomes 

00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:20.734
by the end of my scientific career.

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:26.307
[Music reaches a crescendo, then fades out]

00:02:26.307 --> 00:02:27.730
[Silence]

